body: "## Summary\n`context --for handoff` tells an agent to recover omitted sections using depth flags, but replaying those flags leaves the same sections omitted.\n\nReproduced with the official installed `@unbrained/pm-cli` 2026.9.30 on Linux, using disposable synthetic non-Git trackers. No implementation changes are included in this issue.\n\n## Reproduction\n```bash\nLAB=$(mktemp -d)\nmkdir -p \"$LAB/home\" \"$LAB/work\"\nexport HOME=\"$LAB/home\" PM_PATH=\"$LAB/work/.agents/pm\"\nexport PM_GLOBAL_PATH=\"$LAB/global\" PM_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1\ncd \"$LAB/work\"\npm init \"$PM_PATH\" --defaults --author audit --agent-guidance skip --json >/dev/null\npm create task \"Audit sample\" --id t1 --description \"Execution detail\" --json >/dev/null\n```\n```bash\npm context --for handoff --json >handoff.json\npm context --for handoff --depth deep --json >deep.json\npm context --for handoff --full --json >full.json\npm context --for handoff --section files --json >files.json\n```\n\n## Actual\nThe original response already reports `depth: \"deep\"`, but only includes `activity`, `progress` and `blockers` in `sections_included`. Its omission receipt advertises eight groups:\n- hierarchy, recently_created, unparented and workload: `restore_with: \"--depth standard\"`\n- files, staleness, tests and workspace_memory: `restore_with: \"--depth deep\"`\n\nAdding `--depth deep` or `--full` to the handoff call still yields the same three sections and eight omissions. Explicit `--section files` does restore files. Observed response sizes were 3,396 bytes for a one-task fixture and 3,920 bytes for a 25-task fixture; these are byte measurements, not provider-token counts.\n\n## Expected and impact\nEach restoration instruction must work in the effective invocation that emitted it, including its intent preset. A caller following the current receipt spends another round trip and still lacks the advertised context.\n\n## Implementation plan\n1. Resolve intent, explicit depth/full, explicit section selection and budgets once, with documented precedence.\n2. Build omission receipts from the effective section plan, not a generic depth-to-section table.\n3. Emit a section-specific restoration or a complete corrected invocation when retaining the intent overrides a depth flag.\n4. Distinguish sections omitted by projection/intent from empty sections and from data unavailable under policy or budget.\n5. Integrate these semantics with the coherent coverage/receipt work in #1361; keep adapters thin.\n\n## Regression acceptance\n- [ ] Execute every emitted restore instruction against its originating invocation and assert the named section is restored, or a truthful remaining constraint is reported\n- [ ] Cover handoff/inspect/execute presets × depth/full/section options\n- [ ] Cover empty and populated files/tests/workspace-memory sections and 1/25-task fixtures\n- [ ] Preserve effective tracker, filters, format, visibility policy and budget in recovery\n- [ ] No endless identical omission/recovery loop; empty data is not misrepresented as hidden data\n\n## Ownership and duplicate check\nChild of #1363; concrete receipt case for #1361 and #1362 under #1357. Searched open and closed handoff/restore_with issues. #1351 concerns get's empty children omission; this distinct context-intent precedence failure is not that issue."
